  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $f(x)\\in [-1,1].$ So, for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 3x-2 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+2}{3}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that\n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y+2}{3})=3(\\frac{y+2}{3})-2=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2 & =3{x}_{2}-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $h$ has two roots, $x=0,1.$ So, we have $h(0)=0=h(1),$ while $0\\ne 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
